We present a novel rational method for identifying multiple types of dynamic behavior,
including stable and traveling chimera states, asynchronously synchronized states,
and volatile states within networks of coupled phase oscillators. This approach can
be applied to a broad range of network architectures and utilizes a minor adjustment
to the nodes' natural frequencies, offering a significant advantage over existing
identification methods that are generally confined to specific state types and
network structures. Furthermore, we enhance the method by introducing an
optimization technique that minimizes a cost function, enabling the location of
the most volatile states within these networks. We validate our approach through
numerical simulations and illustrate its applicability to complex multiband
chimera states. Additionally, we explore the method's performance under different
network topologies and oscillator natural frequency distributions. Lastly, we
analyze the effects of parameter variations, network rearrangements, and the role
of chimera state stability on our proposed approach. Overall, our method
establishes a robust framework for investigating multi-chimera states and
corresponding volatile states in networks of coupled phase oscillators. Flick to
