This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. This investigation is an extension of a related study in reference [J. Zhang, J. Differential Equations 271(1), 2021]. Moreover, this research is motivated by the observations and results presented in references [G. Berman, J. Nonlinear Anal. Realworld Appl. 11, 2020]; [G. Berman, J. Nonlinear Anal. Realworld Appl. 12, 2021]; and [G. Berman, J. Nonlinear Anal. Realworld Appl. 13, 2022]. 

In the context of $\sigma$-symmetry, this research also confirms the uniqueness of normalized ground state solutions for the given equation. This result is derived from an application of an approach introduced in reference [Z. Q. Wang, J. Nonlinear Anal. 18(3), 2020] and incorporates specific $\sigma$-steplength conditions. 

