The concept of localization in networks is highly debated, as its measurement depends on the method employed. We investigated nodal activity in dynamic processes occurring on various networks by analyzing the distribution of nodal activators during these processes. Our results demonstrated that nodal activity displays a distinct pattern, essentially unaffected by the system's stationary distribution. We examined different dynamic processes, including the classic Becker-Doring model and a general activator-inhibitor system, and found that the results can be interpreted through the lens of node degree in homogeneous networks or specific differential equations in network heterogeneities. Our findings have significant implications for research on dynamic processes and the localization of these processes on networks. We have made our analytical approach and network generator code publicly available to facilitate replication and extension of our results. Furthermore, we illustrate potential applications of our nodal activity measure by investigating the impact of node removal on dynamic processes and identifying 'critical' nodes that significantly influence the process. Our approach provides a novel framework for studying dynamic processes and can be adapted to any system characterized by a set of ordinary differential equations. We have used this framework to analyze multiple research scenarios and provide results that can be used as benchmarks in future studies. Benchmark models examined in this study are comparable to models analyzed in notable works such as [1] and [2]. We believe that our approach to measuring nodal activity will contribute to a deeper understanding and more robust analysis of dynamic processes occurring within complex networks. 

